        title: 'Fire Safety Preparedness: What Every Security Guard Must Know',
        excerpt: 'From identifying fire hazards to operating extinguishers and coordinating evacuations, this essential guide covers fire safety protocols that every security professional should master.',
        imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1516549655169-df83a0774514?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
        content: `
            <p>Fire safety is one of the most critical responsibilities for security personnel deployed at commercial, industrial, and residential sites. A security guard is often the first responder when a fire breaks out, and their actions in the initial minutes can mean the difference between a controlled situation and a catastrophic disaster.</p>

            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mt-4 mb-2">Understanding Fire Triangle and Common Causes</h3>
            <p>Every fire requires three elements: heat, fuel, and oxygen — known as the fire triangle. In commercial settings, common fire causes include electrical short circuits, overloaded power strips, unattended cooking equipment in pantries, improperly stored flammable materials, and cigarette disposal near combustible areas.</p>
            <p>As a security guard, your daily patrol should include checking for these hazards. Report any exposed wiring, blocked fire exits, missing extinguishers, or storage of flammable materials near heat sources immediately to the site supervisor.</p>

            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mt-4 mb-2">Types of Fire Extinguishers and Their Uses</h3>
            <p>Not all fires are the same, and using the wrong extinguisher can make things worse. Security guards must understand the four main classes:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mt-2">
                <li><strong>Class A (Water/Foam):</strong> For ordinary combustibles like wood, paper, and cloth.</li>
                <li><strong>Class B (CO2/Dry Chemical):</strong> For flammable liquids like petrol, diesel, and solvents.</li>
                <li><strong>Class C (CO2/Dry Chemical):</strong> For electrical fires involving wiring, servers, and electrical panels.</li>
                <li><strong>Class D (Dry Powder):</strong> For combustible metals — rare but found in manufacturing environments.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mt-2">Remember the <strong>PASS technique</strong>: Pull the pin, Aim at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle, and Sweep from side to side.</p>

            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mt-4 mb-2">Evacuation Coordination</h3>
            <p>In a fire emergency, the security guard's primary responsibility is the safe evacuation of all building occupants. This involves activating the fire alarm system, directing people to designated emergency exits, assisting persons with disabilities, preventing people from using elevators, and ensuring assembly at designated muster points.</p>
            <p>Never allow anyone to re-enter the building until the fire department declares it safe. Maintain a headcount at the assembly point and report any missing persons to emergency services immediately.</p>

            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mt-4 mb-2">Regular Drill Participation</h3>
            <p>At Unique Delta Force, fire safety drills are conducted quarterly at every client site. Guards participate in simulated evacuation exercises, extinguisher operation practice, and coordination drills with local fire stations. This consistent training ensures that when a real emergency occurs, the response is swift, organized, and effective.</p>

            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 italic">Unique Delta Force provides certified fire safety training for all deployed guards as part of our standard service package. Contact us to learn more about our comprehensive safety programs.</p>
        `
