        title: 'How to Report a Security Incident: A Step-by-Step Guide for Guards',
        excerpt: 'Accurate and timely incident reporting is as important as the response itself. This guide teaches security guards how to document incidents professionally for legal and operational purposes.',
        imageUrl: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1586075010923-2dd4570fb338?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
        content: `
            <p>Every security guard will encounter incidents during their career — from minor access violations to serious criminal events. How these incidents are documented can determine the outcome of investigations, insurance claims, legal proceedings, and future prevention strategies. Professional incident reporting is a core skill that separates trained security professionals from untrained watchmen.</p>

            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mt-4 mb-2">Why Incident Reports Matter</h3>
            <p>An incident report serves multiple critical purposes. It creates a legal record of what happened, providing documentation that can be used in court proceedings or insurance claims. It enables analysis by helping security managers identify patterns and vulnerabilities. It ensures accountability by showing that the security team responded appropriately. And it facilitates communication between the security agency, the client, and law enforcement.</p>

            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mt-4 mb-2">The 5 W's Framework</h3>
            <p>Every incident report should answer these five questions clearly and completely:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mt-2">
                <li><strong>Who:</strong> Who was involved? Names, descriptions, employee IDs, or physical descriptions if identity is unknown.</li>
                <li><strong>What:</strong> What exactly happened? Describe the events in chronological order using factual, objective language.</li>
                <li><strong>When:</strong> Exact date and time of the incident, including when you became aware of it and when you responded.</li>
                <li><strong>Where:</strong> Precise location — building name, floor number, room number, or area description.</li>
                <li><strong>Why/How:</strong> What were the apparent causes or circumstances? Avoid speculation — report only what you observed or what was reported to you.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mt-4 mb-2">Writing Tips for Clear Reports</h3>
            <p>Use simple, direct language. Write in chronological order. Stick to facts, avoiding opinions, assumptions, or exaggerations. Use specific times rather than vague terms like "sometime in the evening." Include names and designations of all persons involved, including witnesses. If quoting someone, use their exact words in quotation marks.</p>

            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mt-4 mb-2">What to Include as Supporting Evidence</h3>
            <p>Attach or reference any available supporting materials: CCTV footage timestamps and camera numbers, photographs of damage, injuries, or the scene, visitor log entries, patrol register entries, radio communication logs, and statements from witnesses.</p>

            <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mt-4 mb-2">Submission and Follow-Up</h3>
            <p>At Unique Delta Force, incident reports must be submitted to the site supervisor within 2 hours of the incident. The supervisor reviews the report, adds observations, and forwards it to the Area Manager. Critical incidents are escalated to the client and senior management within 4 hours. Digital copies are stored in our centralized incident management system for analysis and future reference.</p>

            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 italic">All Unique Delta Force guards receive hands-on training in professional incident reporting during their induction program. Contact us to learn about our training standards.</p>
        `
